# Implement the CLI and one-command local setup

## Goal

Make one installed `protonmail-ai` binary usable directly by a person and easy
to register as a local MCP server.

## Required work

- Implement `auth`, `mail`, `mcp`, `doctor`, and `serve` command groups, with
  remote behavior remaining disabled until the remote-service task is complete.
- Keep one application service layer behind CLI and MCP transports.
- Provide a one-command installation path with integrity verification.
- Provide a one-action MCP-client setup path that resolves the installed binary
  and writes or prints secret-free configuration.
- Make configuration precedence, account selection, and diagnostics explicit.

## Acceptance criteria

- One documented command installs the complete product.
- CLI and MCP produce equivalent results for the same accepted operation.
- Local usage needs no cloud account or hosted intermediary.
- Installation, upgrade, and removal are exercised on claimed platforms.
- From a clean supported machine, a user can install, log in, run `doctor`,
  register the MCP server, and invoke a read-only tool without editing JSON.

## Evidence

- End-to-end CLI/MCP equivalence tests
- Fresh-machine installation transcript
- Redacted login, doctor, client-setup, and first-tool transcript

## Existing compose-url diagnostic safety

- The explicit human `mail compose-url` helper now redacts all recipients,
  subject, and body content from `ComposeInput` Debug output. Unknown CLI
  options and unreadable body-file paths are no longer echoed through
  `ComposeInputError` Debug or CLI stderr. Red-first synthetic CLI and
  direct-format tests demonstrated both leaks before the fix, while valid
  opt-in compose URL generation and its existing encoding tests still pass.

- The CLI now reads OS-native argument bytes before UTF-8 conversion and
  rejects malformed text with a single content-free diagnostic. Previously
  `std::env::args()` panicked before the compose parser's redaction boundary.
  Red-first process evidence verifies a malformed recipient exits normally,
  emits no URL, and never echoes the secret argument or a panic backtrace.

- Human `mail compose-url --body-file` input now refuses FIFOs and device
  entries before reading content. The descriptor is opened nonblocking, and
  its actual file type is checked, so a FIFO with no writer cannot trap the
  CLI before the 16 KiB read bound. A bounded red-first child-process test
  proves the previous indefinite wait and the fail-closed regular-file gate.

- Compose `--to`, `--cc`, `--bcc`, and `--subject` now apply Unicode-aware
  single-line validation. The old ASCII-byte guard silently accepted U+0085
  NEL and U+2028/U+2029 separators, which can create additional displayed
  lines despite a single-line field. Red-first synthetic process evidence
  covers recipients and subjects while ordinary Unicode remains supported.

- Compose recipient and subject validation also rejects hidden Unicode
  bidirectional-direction formatting such as U+202E and U+2066/U+2069.
  Red-first synthetic CLI tests demonstrated that those markers previously
  passed through in URL-encoded addresses and subjects without warnings.
  All four single-line fields now refuse the controls with redacted errors;
  ordinary printable Unicode and multiline body text remain unchanged.
